# Traducción al inglés: páginas del Manual (metodos.py, parte 2).
T = {
    "a96669b055": "  2010  DESeq (Anders & Huber): differential expression of counts",
    "598fff8b8a": "\"With transcriptomics we may lose resolution, but we gain perspective.\"",
    "ae022930c5": "An ordered set of probes of known sequence on a positively charged surface (glass, plastic, silicon); DNA is negatively charged.",
    "331faa05f4": "  • <titulo>cDNA</> probes: known genes (GenBank), long (0.6-2.5 kb).",
    "64ca65b643": "  • <titulo>Oligonucleotide</> probes: short (25-50 bases).",
    "9da4a519aa": "Workflow: isolate RNA from samples A and B → cDNA labeled with fluorophores (e.g. red and green) → hybridize on the array → scan: <mal>red</> A > B, <bien>green</> B > A, <titulo>yellow</> A = B.",
    "a3d0792d35": "Applications: gene expression, mutations, SNPs, splicing (with special oligos).",
    "b0c41d803f": "                         Microarrays      RNA-seq",
    "fda1e377b1": "  Principle              hybridization    sequencing",
    "fe382dd333": "  De novo (no genome)    no               yes",
    "1277a6ae9f": "  Isoforms               limited          yes",
    "9ad0eb4606": "  New genes              no               yes",
    "68287e8395": "  Strand-specific        no               yes",
    "df1fd75630": "  Reproducible           yes              yes",
    "15862bf02c": "  Cost                   medium           high (computing)",
    "6b173d6533": "  Analysis complexity    medium           high",
    "04ab21b0b3": "<bien>Microarrays</>: simple protocol and analysis, less storage, cheap if a good array already exists. <mal>But</>: cross-hybridization, you only see what has a probe, lower dynamic range (low signals blend into background, high ones saturate).",
    "50f76721a8": "<bien>RNA-seq</>: high sensitivity and dynamic range, no cross-hybridization, discovers transcripts and isoforms, works without a genome (de novo). <mal>But</>: more bioinformatic complexity, data and computing.",
    "50c53dd499": "Sequencing all transcripts in a sample with massive (NGS) sequencing.",
    "364e0983de": "  1. Extract total RNA (mRNA, sncRNA such as miRNA, lncRNA, rRNA).",
    "fdb2086805": "  2. Synthesize cDNA and build libraries.",
    "a3877056c7": "  3. Sequence: millions of short reads from cDNA fragments.",
    "7622645e18": "Applications:",
    "b62c5dedd6": "  • Discover coding and non-coding genes (mapping to a reference or de novo).",
    "9c86ecffec": "  • Annotate genomes: correct gene models, find unpredicted genes.",
    "d90482d013": "  • Alternative splicing: isoforms, alternative exons, exon skipping.",
    "5542b9fdea": "  • Mutations and SNPs in expressed regions.",
    "ede4729ed1": "  • Expression profiles (transcriptional atlases); comparing tissues, stages or conditions.",
    "ea8808603f": "  • Differentially expressed genes.",
    "e3cc15c7eb": "Perspectives: more throughput and lower cost, integration of short and long reads, single cell, reproducible methods.",
    "cfa3cc5dfa": "Platforms seen in class: Ion Torrent · 454/FLX+ · MiSeq · HiSeq/NextSeq/NovaSeq (Illumina) · PacBio · Nanopore.",
    "95e3cc6741": "  <agua>Illumina</>   synthesis with reversible terminators; short reads (50-300 nt), very accurate, hundreds of millions to billions per run. MiSeq: few samples / amplicons; NextSeq: medium; NovaSeq: large projects.",
    "9577caf163": "  <agua>Ion Torrent</> detects the H⁺ released when a nucleotide is incorporated; 200-600 nt reads; errors in homopolymers.",
    "8b6b3689d1": "  <agua>454</>         pyrosequencing; long reads for its time; discontinued.",
    "7f58d484b3": "Isolation of total RNA (cell, animal or plant tissue):",
    "dca918fe91": "  <titulo>Homogenization</> → <titulo>Lysis</> → <titulo>Separation</> → <titulo>Precipitation</>",
    "0ee3d55c55": "Before measuring expression you must make sure the RNA is <titulo!>intact, pure and free of contaminating DNA</>.",
    "2f984dfab6": "  • <agua>Electrophoresis</>: sharp rRNA bands (28S/18S in animals, 26S/18S in plants). Smear = degradation; band at the top = genomic DNA.",
    "2e8cf88d27": "  • <agua>Spectrophotometry</>: A260/A280 = 1.8-2.0 (protein) and A260/A230 = 1.8-2.0 (salts, phenol, guanidine, carbohydrates).",
    "2efa7271c4": "  • <agua>Bioanalyzer</> (microfluidics): electropherogram and <titulo>RIN</> from 0 (degraded) to 10 (intact). Above 8 is considered suitable for RNA-seq.",
    "7c9e0e188c": "Every design starts by writing the question in one line: <titulo>what</> is compared, <titulo>in what</> (organism, tissue, cell type) and <titulo>what is measured</> (gene level, isoform, splicing, miRNA, single cell, location).",
    "eef66b6d94": "The question decides the library, read type, depth and analysis:",
    "251ac3b98f": "  • How much does each gene change? → bulk poly(A) or 3′ tag, SE, 10-30 M.",
    "6b9d765423": "  • Which isoforms/exons? → PE 2×100-150, 40-100 M, or long reads.",
    "cd64f94202": "  • lncRNA, antisense? → ribodepleted total RNA WITH strand.",
    "ba1e422565": "  • miRNA? → small RNA-seq.",
    "a4ca22e15a": "  • Which cell type changes? → single cell with several donors.",
    "30439bf54a": "  • Where in the tissue? → spatial.",
    "6f65e19dc6": "  • Synthesis or translation? → nascent RNA, 4sU, Ribo-seq.",
    "fe3005684f": "Also define the experimental unit: what receives the treatment independently (a mouse, a patient, a separately seeded culture). That is your replicate.",
    "38c491ae5c": "<titulo!>Biological replicate</>: another independent experimental unit (another mouse, another patient, another culture seeded and treated separately). It captures the variation that matters for generalizing.",
    "d095bc1c5b": "<titulo!>Technical replicate</>: the same sample measured again. In RNA-seq technical variation is nearly Poisson and small compared with biological (Marioni 2008): it is almost never worth it.",
    "605e586609": "How many?",
    "33e7745f5a": "  • Absolute minimum: 3 per group (without replicates there is no valid p-value).",
    "37d47e211e": "  • Schurch 2016 (48 yeast replicates): with 3, only 20-40 % of DE genes are detected; recommends <bien!>≥ 6</>, and <bien!>≥ 12</> if small changes matter.",
    "ba876088f6": "  • Humans and tissues vary more than cell lines: more replicates.",
    "6995a75d27": "<mal!>Pseudoreplication</>: treating as independent things that are not (wells from the same culture, cells from the same donor, technical replicates). It shrinks the variance and manufactures false positives.",
    "a27fcf2b26": "More reads improve the measurement of lowly expressed genes, but with diminishing returns.",
    "8e6956a722": "Liu 2014 (MCF7, 7 replicates): beyond ~10 M reads almost no DE genes are gained; going from 2 to 3 replicates does (+35 %).",
    "32a9bb0c75": "Practical guide (reads per sample):",
    "64cd37a256": "  • Genes, well-annotated organism: 10-30 M (ENCODE asks for 30 M aligned)",
    "242f00232e": "  • lncRNA, lowly expressed genes: 40-60 M",
    "6a09c7e3a4": "  • Isoforms and splicing: 50-100 M, paired",
    "b24c15ab14": "  • De novo: 30-60 M paired per sample",
    "04617ce395": "  • Single cell: ≥ 20,000 reads per cell",
    "44af28d272": "A saturation curve (subsample reads and count detected genes) tells whether more depth would help.",
    "910635a02c": "<titulo>Power</> is the probability of detecting a real change. It depends on: effect size (fold change), number of replicates, biological variation (dispersion), the gene's counts (depth × expression) and the threshold (FDR).",
    "8304f9c6e0": "Conesa 2016 (gene with 70 reads, α 5 %), power by replicates 3 / 5 / 10:",
    "c7c2bc4f71": "  1.25× change:  17 % /  25 % /  44 %",
    "51c6431cf1": "  1.5× change :  43 % /  64 % /  91 %",
    "739a74f03d": "  2× change   :  87 % /  98 % / 100 %",
    "4ca0cb2879": "Tools: RNASeqPower, Scotty, PROPER, ssizeRNA. Use the dispersion from a pilot or from similar public data (same tissue and species).",
    "56a6fa35ec": "At the design desk the game shows you an approximate power (not in Hard).",
    "908dc93481": "A <titulo>batch</> is everything processed together: extraction day, kit, person, sequencing run, lane. Each batch leaves a footprint on the expression of thousands of genes.",
    "e5333254ea": "If batches coincide with groups, the batch passes for biology (<mal!>confounding</>): no method fixes it afterwards.",
    "4127f0aee3": "  <mal!>BAD</>     Monday: C C C C C C   Tuesday: G G G G G G",
    "f160f6a9f5": "  <bien!>GOOD</>    Monday: C G G C C G   Tuesday: G C G C C G",
    "585c580cf3": "Rules:",
    "36837ffd47": "  1. Block: each batch with samples from all groups, in equal numbers.",
    "38b758fbd5": "  2. Randomize the order within each batch.",
    "638c528d2b": "  3. Record EVERYTHING (date, kit, operator, lane) on the sample sheet.",
    "264c92b9a3": "  4. Put the batch in the formula: ~ batch + condition.",
    "3cb6fb7a8a": "  5. Look at the PCA colored by batch before trusting a result.",
    "dd0ba9af5f": "If you don't know the batches: SVA or RUVSeq estimate hidden factors. ComBat-seq corrects the counts (for visualization; for testing the formula is better).",
    "5e29e44658": "Fisher's three principles that Auer & Doerge 2010 brought to RNA-seq:",
    "d64d0aefe4": "  <titulo!>Replication</>: several units per group to estimate variation.",
    "df5a3cf42e": "  <titulo!>Randomization</>: chance decides which unit gets which treatment and in what order it is processed. It protects against biases you don't know about.",
    "6350781fe7": "  <titulo!>Blocking</>: group similar units (same day, same litter, same patient) and compare within the block.",
    "281c374718": "In sequencing: with barcodes (multiplexing) all samples go together in every lane; if you use several lanes, each sample in all of them (or a balanced design). That way lane is not confounded with anything.",
    "3f7b6a4c56": "The formula tells the model what explains each gene's expression. The <titulo>last term</> is the one tested by default in DESeq2.",
    "bc989c804d": "  ~ condition                  two or more groups, nothing else",
    "6f674d3c5a": "  ~ batch + condition          condition adjusted for batch",
    "9c60d42a92": "  ~ donor + condition          paired: each donor their own control",
    "e80e4a5228": "  ~ genotype * treatment       factorial with interaction",
    "a99ca1cb9b": "  ~ time (factor) + LRT        does it change at any time?",
    "163313d3a3": "  • A factor that is an exact combination of others makes the matrix singular (\"model matrix is not full rank\"): it happens with confounded batches.",
    "2a1ca8b124": "  • Each coefficient needs degrees of freedom: n − parameters > 0.",
    "eadd156b91": "  • Continuous covariates (age, RIN) can be included, centered.",
    "e055870e48": "R code (DESeq2):",
    "8fb76ae374": "If each individual gives one sample of each condition (tumor and normal; before and after), the difference is measured within the individual.",
    "81ca11c13e": "Variation between people (genetics, age, diet) is usually larger than the effect you are after; putting it in the model removes it from the error:",
    "a2932778e3": "If you ignore the pairing, the variation between people stays as noise and you lose power. And if a patient has only one of the two samples, it contributes little to the contrast but does to the dispersion.",
    "cb81ae31b4": "Two crossed factors (genotype × treatment) give four groups.",
    "c7974e185a": "  treatment effect in WT  = WT.trt − WT.ctrl",
    "feb578c410": "  treatment effect in KO  = KO.trt − KO.ctrl",
    "73a1440d51": "  <titulo!>interaction</>             = (KO.trt − KO.ctrl) − (WT.trt − WT.ctrl)",
    "e6c3ce2d31": "Equivalent alternative: a \"group\" factor with 4 levels (~ group) and the numeric contrast of the difference of differences.",
    "8657d7da65": "Without interaction (~ genotype + treatment) you assume the treatment does the same in both genotypes. The interaction needs more replicates: it is a difference of four means.",
    "aef047f735": "RNA degrades within minutes (RNases). What happens between the animal and the freezer decides the quality.",
    "5923c0d0ad": "  • Short ischemia time, constant between samples (record it).",
    "dd345d5507": "  • Freeze in liquid nitrogen, or RNAlater (penetrates ~5 mm), or TRIzol directly. Store at −80 °C.",
    "b86b188ab8": "  • Same time of day for all (circadian rhythm), same fasting.",
    "218c14ae40": "  • Dissociation for single cell: fast and cold; it induces stress genes (FOS, JUN): consider nuclei or cold dissociation.",
    "47443ec2f7": "  • FFPE: standardized fixation; cut the block fresh.",
    "62169693c2": "  • Sample sheet from day 1: ID, group, sex, age, date, operator, batch, RIN… it is your colData.",
    "6db5956cd7": "Methods:",
    "4e52101c6b": "  • Phenol-chloroform (TRIzol): high yield, keeps small RNAs.",
    "b0aa366eb1": "  • Silica columns (RNeasy): clean, loses < 200 nt except miRNA kits.",
    "2759c946e7": "  • Magnetic beads: automatable.",
    "cce63ea956": "<titulo>DNase I</> treatment (on column or afterwards): genomic DNA contaminates mostly total-RNA libraries.",
    "932969fd96": "RNase-free work: gloves, filter tips, clean surfaces, nuclease-free water.",
    "a8b6dacc2f": "Extract all samples with the same kit and, if they don't fit in one day, in balanced batches.",
    "3350c6baee": "<titulo!>Quantity</>: Qubit (fluorescence, RNA-specific) better than NanoDrop (absorbance, counts DNA and salts).",
    "f1506cd3d7": "<titulo!>Purity</> (NanoDrop): A260/280 ≈ 2.0 (low protein); A260/230 > 1.8 (no phenol, guanidine or carbohydrates).",
    "125b4f28f2": "<titulo!>Integrity</> (Bioanalyzer / TapeStation):",
    "63adeaa365": "  • RIN (1-10) uses the 28S/18S peaks and the shape of the electropherogram. Poly(A) asks for RIN ≥ 7-8.",
    "aaf0991419": "  • DV200 = % of fragments > 200 nt. For FFPE the RIN is useless; DV200 ≥ 30 % is the usual minimum (Illumina), 30-50 %: more input.",
    "30d9debdec": "If RIN differs systematically between groups, it is a disguised batch: treat it as a covariate.",
    "e93172cd25": "Steps of a typical Illumina library:",
    "882dfe031c": "  1. Enrich: oligo-dT (poly(A)) or remove rRNA (ribodepletion).",
    "39858ba709": "  2. Fragment the RNA (heat + Mg²⁺) to ~200-500 nt.",
    "c3d831dd21": "  3. First-strand cDNA with random hexamers (bias in the first bases: Hansen 2010).",
    "0c2b487ecb": "  4. Second strand (with dUTP if stranded).",
    "94cf95a74d": "  5. End repair, A-tailing, ligation of adapters with indexes (barcodes), UMIs if applicable.",
    "0a145551f4": "  6. Degrade the U strand (UDG) if stranded.",
    "6c16dc1bfd": "  7. Low-cycle PCR (as few as possible).",
    "e8e5c79f9a": "  8. Clean-up and size selection with beads.",
    "79137de15c": "Choose (see Catalog): poly(A) · ribodepletion · 3′ tag · small RNA · single cell · long reads · spatial.",
    "e59108e30e": "ERCC spike-ins: added to the RNA (or per number of cells) before library preparation.",
    "1f0ccb6f1e": "Without strand information, a read in a region with genes on both strands is ambiguous.",
    "7edff7aaab": "dUTP method: the second strand carries dUTP and is degraded before PCR; only the information of the original strand survives (Levin 2010 rated it the best of those evaluated).",
    "edc05c346b": "With dUTP, read 1 is antisense to the RNA: in featureCounts -s 2, in HTSeq --stranded=reverse, in Salmon libType ISR (detected automatically with -l A).",
    "b52ed01b04": "Check it with RSeQC infer_experiment.py: ~90 % or more on one side.",
    "d5cf980de7": "Essential for antisense lncRNAs, overlapping genes, de novo. Almost free in modern kits: use it whenever you can.",
    "10d8df9932": "A <titulo>UMI</> is a random sequence attached to each molecule BEFORE PCR. Two reads with the same position and the same UMI are PCR copies; with different UMIs they are different molecules.",
    "a3f38b195f": "Without UMIs, removing duplicates by coordinates erases real signal from highly expressed genes (Fu 2018): in normal bulk RNA-seq you do NOT deduplicate.",
    "2113d0521a": "Useful with little material, FFPE, small RNA and single cell (where they are standard). Tool: UMI-tools (corrects sequencing errors in the UMI).",
    "28397e9e2b": "92 synthetic poly(A) RNAs, 250-2,000 nt, at known concentrations spanning ~10⁶-fold (NIST SRM 2374). Mixes 1 and 2 with known ratios between subgroups.",
    "f45191d337": "Uses:",
    "341e8c42a8": "  • QC: linearity of counts vs concentration, detection limit, accuracy of fold changes.",
    "865d44e61a": "  • Normalization when total RNA per cell changes (Lovén 2012, MYC): they are added in proportion to the NUMBER OF CELLS, not to RNA mass.",
    "70e5d4e2f8": "In DESeq2: estimateSizeFactors(dds, controlGenes = is_ercc). In RUVSeq: RUVg with the ERCCs as negative controls.",
    "2bc62f73d6": "Library QC: size (Bioanalyzer, peak ~300-400 bp), molar concentration (KAPA qPCR); no adapter dimers.",
    "de04e3fc3e": "Equimolar pooling of all indexed samples; use unique dual indexes (UDI) to avoid index hopping.",
    "9b75604f27": "Illumina (synthesis with reversible terminators): clusters form on the flow cell and one base is read each cycle. PhiX at 1-5 % as a control (more if the library has low diversity, e.g. small RNA or amplicons).",
    "bc7e29fc9a": "Plan lanes: total reads = samples × depth / (1 − losses).",
    "033c6378e0": "Demultiplex with bcl-convert/bcl2fastq → one FASTQ per sample (R1, R2).",
    "9a121cfaf7": "  SE 50-75   genes in well-annotated organisms, small RNA, 3′ tag",
    "534c49fe92": "  PE 2×100   isoforms, splicing, better mapping in repetitive regions",
    "7b3aef5c03": "  PE 2×150   de novo, fusions, variants",
    "fa31de94b9": "  Long       full-length isoforms (PacBio/ONT)",
    "d6346541f9": "Paired: both ends of the same fragment; they help place ambiguous reads and see junctions. For differential gene expression the gain is small relative to the cost.",
    "a799b966a0": "FastQC per sample and MultiQC to see them all together. What to look at:",
    "a3ab8bbdaf": "  • Per-position quality (Phred ≥ 30 = 1 error in 1,000; it usually drops at the end).",
    "45705b73b1": "  • Per-base content: the first ~10 bases oscillate due to hexamer bias (normal in RNA-seq).",
    "ec3604efcf": "  • Duplication: HIGH is normal in RNA-seq (highly expressed genes).",
    "810edd6e40": "  • Overrepresented sequences: adapters, rRNA, dimers.",
    "74f495530a": "  • GC per read: a second hump suggests contamination.",
    "8aa037ee17": "What matters: that all samples look alike (Conesa: discard those that differ > 30 %).",
    "4ee182a603": "Remove adapters (if the fragment is shorter than the read) and low-quality ends; discard very short reads.",
    "5f86e7f391": "For aligners with soft-clipping (STAR) or pseudoaligners, aggressive trimming helps little; adapter trimming is necessary in small RNA.",
    "77339e2afe": "<titulo!>Against the genome</> (spliced): STAR or HISAT2 → BAM → counts per gene with featureCounts/HTSeq. Allows coverage QC and junction discovery.",
    "2ba515536a": "<titulo!>Against the transcriptome</> (pseudoalignment): Salmon / kallisto, very fast; they estimate per-transcript counts with EM and correct GC and positional bias. Summarize to genes with tximport.",
    "b780a738ea": "ALWAYS use the same genome and the same annotation (Ensembl/GENCODE version) for all samples; record it in the methods.",
    "42efe28660": "Expected in human: 70-90 % of reads aligned to the genome.",
    "cbb630d2ad": "RSeQC, Qualimap, Picard CollectRnaSeqMetrics. What to look at:",
    "4c76e9ef9b": "  • % aligned (70-90 % in human), % multimapping.",
    "d92d22058c": "  • Distribution: exons / introns / intergenic (poly(A): mostly exonic; total RNA: more introns).",
    "1891843292": "  • Coverage along the gene: 3′ bias = degraded RNA (especially poly(A)).",
    "5107521d2d": "  • Strand (infer_experiment.py) correct for your kit.",
    "f81384663c": "  • % rRNA and % mitochondrial.",
    "647bf02e9c": "  • Saturation: would more reads give more genes?",
    "52560e2644": "After counting: correlation between replicates (ENCODE: Spearman > 0.9 isogenic, > 0.8 between donors), PCA, hierarchical clustering.",
    "7909ed5cbc": "Counts depend on depth, on each library's composition and on gene length.",
    "fb72dfb83f": "<titulo!>Within a sample</> (comparing genes with each other): RPKM/FPKM and TPM. TPM sums to the same in all samples; useful for plots, NOT for count models.",
    "d52424c4d8": "<titulo!>Between samples</> (comparing a gene across conditions):",
    "d1afa7578a": "  • DESeq2: median of ratios (size factor = median of count / gene's geometric mean).",
    "2cd5cfbf2f": "  • edgeR: TMM (trimmed mean of the log-ratios).",
    "cf80391b59": "  • Both assume that MOST genes do not change.",
    "e6e6dc8faf": "  • If total RNA per cell changes: spike-ins (Lovén 2012).",
    "58f12963f3": "  • Total counts (CPM) fail when a few genes hog the reads.",
    "7ceb6a8f5e": "For plots and PCA: a variance-stabilizing transformation (vst, rlog) or log-CPM.",
    "15847884ab": "Before any test:",
    "51fd3a6b53": "What to look for:",
    "45b96bd9e8": "  • Do replicates cluster by condition?",
    "e7bf50d379": "  • Does PC1 separate by batch, sex, RIN or date? → covariate in the formula.",
    "6964707694": "  • Any sample far from all the others? → check its QC before removing it (never just because \"it doesn't look nice\").",
    "ec5d35f2c8": "  • Heatmap of distances between samples.",
    "30f4e1ac5d": "In this game, the results PCA colors each sample by group and labels it with its batch number.",
    "2674c5ad57": "Counts are <titulo>negative binomial</>: variance = μ + α·μ². With 3-6 replicates each gene's α is poorly estimated, so DESeq2, edgeR and limma share information across genes (shrinkage / empirical Bayes).",
    "4f75ec2bf0": "  • DESeq2: Wald for one coefficient; LRT to compare models (time, >2 groups). Detects outliers with Cook's distance.",
    "af9f0e4e09": "  • limma-voom: log-CPM with weights; fast, very good with n ≥ 3 and complex designs (and with random effects via duplicateCorrelation/dream).",
    "8765e38c9e": "  • Schurch 2016: with < 12 replicates, edgeR and DESeq2 are the best.",
    "f8da19b4dc": "Avoid: unmoderated per-gene t-tests, fold-change cutoffs without statistics, tests on FPKM.",
    "542384fb27": "With 20,000 genes and p < 0.05, ~1,000 come out \"significant\" even if nothing changes.",
    "d72fba1a91": "  • Bonferroni (p × m): controls the probability of ONE false positive. Too strict for omics.",
    "41f9456e8d": "  • <bien!>Benjamini-Hochberg</>: controls the expected proportion of false positives among the significant ones (FDR). padj < 0.05 = ≤ 5 % false expected in the list.",
    "6b3a70753c": "Independent filtering (DESeq2 does it on its own): removing low-count genes BEFORE correcting increases power without inflating the FDR, because the filter doesn't look at the condition.",
    "78d25300af": "Fold-change threshold: better to test it (results(lfcThreshold = 1)) than to filter afterwards.",
    "4bd96c24f5": "  • <titulo>MA</> (log2FC vs mean counts): low-count genes have huge, noisy fold changes; apeglm shrinks them.",
    "578995fdf4": "  • <titulo>Volcano</> (log2FC vs −log10 p): size and certainty at once.",
    "cdfb7fe911": "  • <titulo>Heatmap</> of the DE genes (vst, centered per gene).",
    "2d7449ee7f": "  • Normalized counts of individual genes per group (plotCounts).",
    "fc03038db2": "Report: number of tested genes, threshold (padj, |log2FC|), direction, and the full table as supplementary material.",
    "73eeb83cf9": "<titulo!>ORA</> (over-representation): does the list of DE genes have more genes from a pathway than expected? Hypergeometric test. The universe must be the TESTED genes, not the genome. Bias: long genes are detected more → goseq corrects it.",
    "14d8baa3fd": "<titulo!>GSEA</>: ranks ALL genes (by statistic or shrunken log2FC) and looks for pathways piled up at one end; needs no threshold (fgsea, clusterProfiler).",
    "aa314c5d06": "Databases: GO (process, function, component), KEGG, Reactome, MSigDB Hallmark.",
    "d44a401b39": "Also correct for multiple testing across pathways.",
    "4a7758745d": "Complements: WGCNA (coexpression modules), deconvolution (CIBERSORTx), transcription factors (DoRothEA/decoupleR).",
    "214e307f89": "Three levels:",
    "eb37f27488": "  • Differential transcript expression (DTE): Salmon + DESeq2/edgeR per transcript (or fishpond's swish).",
    "54050fcbd9": "  • Differential transcript usage (DTU): the PROPORTION of isoforms changes even if the gene total does not (DRIMSeq, DEXSeq on transcripts).",
    "9692f31419": "  • Splicing events: skipped exon, alternative 5′/3′, retained intron, mutually exclusive exons (rMATS: ΔPSI; DEXSeq: exon usage; LeafCutter: introns).",
    "5c7aafdc85": "Requirements: long paired reads, 50-100 M, replicates, genome alignment. Validation: RT-PCR with primers in flanking exons.",
    "a7ce2fcdde": "Without a reference genome:",
    "e542d523e9": "  1. Paired, stranded, good-quality reads; adapter trimming.",
    "423dc613d0": "  2. Assemble with ALL samples together (Trinity; in silico normalization if there are too many reads).",
    "f1cb45e7af": "  3. Evaluate: BUSCO (complete single-copy orthologs), contig N50, % of reads that map back.",
    "f6d502a23c": "  4. Reduce redundancy (CD-HIT, EvidentialGene) and predict ORFs (TransDecoder).",
    "c992385bf4": "  5. Annotate by homology (BLAST/DIAMOND against UniProt, Pfam, Trinotate).",
    "822fcceb41": "  6. Quantify each sample against the assembly (Salmon) and sum at the Trinity \"gene\" level → DESeq2/edgeR.",
    "190443e3c5": "  1. Counts: Cell Ranger / STARsolo / alevin-fry (UMIs per cell).",
    "a0837c3e94": "  2. Per-cell QC: number of UMIs, number of genes, % mitochondrial; doublets (scDblFinder); ambient RNA (SoupX, CellBender).",
    "c1c3e58552": "  3. Normalization (log-normalization or sctransform), variable genes, PCA.",
    "9b87daa1fc": "  4. Sample integration if there is a batch effect (Harmony, scVI).",
    "6e6a2d211a": "  5. Neighbors → clusters (Leiden) → UMAP (only for viewing).",
    "623d43a1d1": "  6. Annotation with markers or references.",
    "47c30d94ef": "  7. <bien!>DE between conditions: pseudobulk</> — sum counts per cell type and per sample, and DESeq2/edgeR with the samples as replicates (muscat).",
    "90e7a2a4ac": "  8. Composition: does the proportion of a type change? (propeller, scCODA).",
    "acf6bc24b3": "<mal!>Classic mistake</>: cell-versus-cell Wilcoxon between conditions treats thousands of cells as replicates → thousands of false positives (Squair 2021).",
    "93666acede": "  • RT-qPCR of some DE genes in new samples (independent biological replicates, not the same ones), following MIQE.",
    "708145d837": "  • Protein (western, ELISA, immunofluorescence) if the conclusion is about protein.",
    "eb66be9914": "  • Functional: knockdown/overexpression of the candidate gene.",
    "5d31fa1709": "  • Reanalysis in public data (GEO, GTEx, TCGA).",
    "a61ee7e00c": "  • Versioned workflow: nf-core/rnaseq (Nextflow) or Snakemake, with containers (Docker/Singularity/conda).",
    "139a2c3052": "  • Record software, genome and annotation versions, and the parameters.",
    "a7c084c706": "  • Analysis code in a repository (git); R's sessionInfo().",
    "1dd8d0d934": "  • Deposition: raw FASTQ in SRA/ENA; counts and metadata in GEO or ArrayExpress, following MINSEQE (design description, protocols, raw and processed data, per-sample metadata).",
    "2ac60b1ddf": "  • Human data: consent and controlled access (dbGaP/EGA) if applicable.",
    "a637f51a48": "The \"Methods catalog\" section of this manual has a card for every technology:",
    "7dc61c0a5b": "  <agua>Hybridization and PCR</>: RT-qPCR, microarrays, NanoString",
    "21d7a1cd9a": "  <agua>Tags</>: SAGE, CAGE, 3′ tag-seq",
    "8540537dc8": "  <agua>Bulk</>: poly(A), ribodepletion, capture, small RNA, dual, metatranscriptomics",
    "4411290dc3": "  <agua>Single cell and spatial</>: droplets, Smart-seq, nuclei, Visium, MERFISH",
    "c5f37103fc": "  <agua>Long reads</>: Iso-Seq, Nanopore",
    "39326022a3": "  <agua>Dynamics and translation</>: GRO/PRO-seq, SLAM-seq, Ribo-seq, CLIP",
    "bef1cadfc3": "Short history: Northern → RT-qPCR → microarrays (1995) and SAGE (1995) → RNA-seq (2008) → single cell → spatial and long reads.",
    "539629fd04": "  <agua>PacBio</>      single molecule in real time; long reads (typically up to 4.5-5 kb); HiFi very accurate.",
    "d85e5ad83f": "  <agua>Nanopore</>    ionic current through a nanopore; very long reads (20-30 kb or much more), direct RNA.",
    "acbe63758f": "Third generation = single-molecule sequencing. The ~16 % error rate belongs to the first versions; current ones are much lower.",
}
